Since $\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that

$$
\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\end{aligned}
$$

for any vectors $\mathbf{v}$ and $\mathbf{w}.$ Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}T(4{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =4T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\ [\begin{aligned}-18 \\ 24\end{aligned}] & =4T({v}_{1})+[\begin{aligned}14 \\ -12\end{aligned}] \\ 4T({v}_{1}) & =[\begin{aligned}-18 \\ 24\end{aligned}]-[\begin{aligned}14 \\ -12\end{aligned}] \\ 4T({v}_{1}) & =[\begin{aligned}-32 \\ 36\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{1}) & =\frac{1}{4}[\begin{aligned}-32 \\ 36\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\begin{aligned}-8 \\ 9\end{aligned}].\end{aligned}
$$
